WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.040
Film Courage: What are some things that someone
should know before becoming a filmmaker?

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:09.650
Matthew Berkowitz, Filmmaker: Be strong, know
what you want to say, take advice, don’t

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:15.519
always take it and just go, make something
happen and just go make something great.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:21.130
At least try to every step of the way, write
something honest every day and do your best

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.829
to put what you want to write on screen.

00:00:23.829 --> 00:00:34.050
Film Courage: You say be strong…is that
meant to resist criticism or can you expand

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.250
on this?

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:40.220
Matthew: You should always be able to take
criticism as an artist.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.920
I think that’s part of the job but I think
if you have an intent and a voice and you

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:50.830
want to say something about…you are telling
a story about characters and why people do

00:00:50.830 --> 00:01:00.710
what they do and you stick to your principles
and the theory and the thesis of your story

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.010
and you hold onto that because you have to
hold onto that for two years.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.580
You have to write a film, you’ve got to
raise the money, you’ve got to shoot the

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:15.460
movie, you’ve got to edit the movie, you’ve
got to make sure this one idea is conceptualized

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:18.560
from the beginning to the end throughout the
entire process.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.880
Film Courage: And then there’s distribution
which is its own beast.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:29.079
Matthew: And that’s a different side of
filmmaking (the distribution), it’s a totally

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:34.069
different side but just from advice to young
filmmakers is know what you want to say and

00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:35.850
do your best to hold onto that every day.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.380
Film Courage: What about in terms of being
on set, do you think you almost have to be

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:47.569
a politician or is it better to be more behind
the scenes because there’s also a downside

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:53.310
to being too much of “the nice guy” because
then someone can take advantage of this or

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.219
they think “I don’t have to take this
guy seriously, he’s super cool.

00:01:56.219 --> 00:01:58.299
Don’t worry…I can just do this.”

00:01:58.299 --> 00:02:03.340
Matthew: That’s kind of what I mean by be
strong.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:08.259
Filmmaking is about managing egos, managing
people, managing your story, managing your

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:09.700
characters, managing a lot of things.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.060
And all of these things are happening at a
rapid pace.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.950
You have to make decisions as a director,
you have to make a hundred decisions in a

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.040
matter in a matter of a minute.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.810
So there’s a lot of politics that get involved.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.860
You want to make sure that your actors are
getting what they want because they understand

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.650
the role, you want to make sure that your
DP knows what he’s doing, you want to make

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.710
sure that your script is still the same.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:36.360
You can’t lose sight of a lot of things
that are playing in place.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:44.550
It’s a difficult way to say you can’t
just be a dictator running your own set.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:52.120
There’s a middle ground between the relationship
that a filmmaker has with the people involved

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:59.820
because at the end of the day it’s a collaboration
of multiple artists especially even when you

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.280
come down to sound design and score.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:07.960
By the time you finish the film you’ve got
80 people-100 people working on it.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:13.910
You’ve got at least 12 hopefully ideally
incredible artists that are putting their

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.600
voice into your film.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.840
So being a director is about kind of curating
all of those voices and maintaining your own

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.470
through the entire process.

